#smallcaps[With] these things laid down in general, I go on to consider #emph[three particular cases arising] from the truth set forth. The first relates to the #emph[thing] itself; the second to its #emph[time] or season; and the last to our #emph[conduct] with regard to preventing the evil we are treating of.

First, then, it may be asked: 1. #emph[How may a man know when he has entered into temptation?] 2\. #emph[What directions are to be given for preventing our entering into temptation?] 3. #emph[What seasons are there in which a man may and ought to fear that an hour of temptation is at hand?]

=== First Inquiry: How a Man May Know He Has Entered into Temptation

Our first inquiry is how a man may know whether he has entered into temptation or not. I say, then:

(1.) When a #emph[man is drawn into any sin], he may be sure that he has entered into temptation. All #emph[sin] comes from temptation (Jas 1:14). Sin is a fruit that comes only from that root. However suddenly or violently a man is surprised in or by any sin, yet it is from some temptation or other that he has been surprised. So the apostle says in Gal 6:1. If a man is surprised, caught in a fault, still he was tempted to it; for the apostle says, “watch yourself, or you also may be tempted,” that is, as that man was when he was surprised, as it were, unawares. Men sometimes take no notice of this, to their great harm. When they are caught in a sin, they set themselves to repent of that sin, but they do not consider the temptation that caused it, so as to set themselves against that too and take care not to enter into it again. That is why they are quickly entangled by it again, though they have the greatest detestation of the sin itself that can be expressed. The man who would really win the victory over any sin must consider his temptations to it and strike at that root. Unless he is delivered from that, he will not be healed.

This is a folly that possesses many who nevertheless have a quick and living sense of sin. They are aware of their #emph[sins], but not of their #emph[temptations]. They are displeased with the bitter fruit, but they cherish the poisonous root. So in the midst of their humbling themselves for sin, they will continue in the ways and the company, and in the pursuit of the ends, that occasioned that sin. Of this I will say more later.

(2.) Temptations have #emph[various degrees]. Some rise to such a height, press on the soul so hard, torment and disquiet it so much, and fight so hard against all opposition made to them, that the man assaulted in this way cannot possibly doubt that he must wrestle with a special power of temptation. When a fever rages, a man knows he is sick, unless his disorder has made him delirious. Men’s lusts, as James tells us, “lure away and entice” them and seduce them to sin. But they do this by themselves, without any special urging, in a quieter, steadier, and calmer way. If they grow violent, if they drive the soul up and down and give it no rest, the soul may know that they have got the help of temptation.

Take an empty vessel and put it into some stream that is running toward the sea. It will certainly be carried there, according to the course and speed of the stream. But let strong winds rise upon it, and it will be driven violently against every bank and rock, until it is broken in pieces and swallowed up by the ocean. Men’s lusts will certainly carry them into eternal ruin (unless they are mortified in the death of Christ), but often without much noise, according to the course of the stream of their corruptions. But let the wind of strong temptations befall them, and they are driven into countless scandalous sins; and so, broken on every side, they are swallowed up in eternity. So it is with men in general, and so it is in particular cases. Hezekiah always had the root of #emph[pride] in him. Yet it did not make him run up and down to show his treasure and his riches until he fell into temptation through the ambassadors of the king of Babylon. David too had that root. Yet he could keep back from numbering the people until Satan rose up and provoked him and urged him to do it. Judas was covetous from the beginning. Yet he did not plot to satisfy his covetousness by selling his Master until the devil entered into him, and by that Judas entered into temptation. The same may be said of Abraham, Jonah, Peter, and the rest. So when any lust or corruption whatever stirs up a tumult and disquiets the soul, and drives it violently toward sin, let the soul know that the lust has got the advantage of some outward temptation, though the soul does not yet see where. Or at least the lust has itself become a special temptation through some incitement or provocation that has befallen it, and it is to be watched more than usual.

(3.) Entering into temptation may be seen in its lesser degrees too. For instance, it is seen when the heart secretly begins to #emph[like the matter of the temptation], and is content to feed it and increase it by any ways it can without downright sin.

In particular, suppose a man begins to have a reputation for piety, wisdom, learning, or the like. He is much spoken of in that way; his heart is tickled to hear it, and his pride and ambition are moved by it. If this man now applies himself with all his strength to the things from which his reputation, esteem, and glory among men spring, with a secret eye to having them increased, he is entering into temptation. If he does not take heed, it will quickly make him a slave of lust. So it was with Jehu. He saw that his reputation for zeal was beginning to spread, and he gained honor by it. Jonadab comes his way, a good and holy man. “Now,” thinks Jehu, “I have an opportunity to grow in the honor of my zeal.” So he calls Jonadab to him and sets to work most earnestly. The things he did were good in themselves, but he had entered into temptation, and he served his lust in everything he did. So it is with many scholars. They find themselves esteemed and favored for their learning. This takes hold of the pride and ambition of their hearts. So they set themselves to study with all diligence day and night, which is a good thing in itself. But they do it to satisfy men’s thoughts and words about them, which they delight in; and so in all they do they make provision for the flesh, to gratify its lusts.

It is true that God often brings light out of this darkness and turns things to a better outcome. Perhaps a man has studied for a number of years with an eye on his lusts, his ambition, pride, and vainglory, rising early and going to bed late to satisfy them. Then God comes in with his grace, turns the soul to himself, plunders those Egyptian lusts, and so consecrates to the use of the tabernacle what was provided for idols.

Men may be entangled in this way in better things than learning, even in the #emph[profession] of piety, in their labor in the ministry, and the like. Some men’s profession is a snare to them. They are well thought of, and much honored on account of their profession and strict walk. This often happens in the days in which we live, when everything is carried on by parties. Some find themselves, on the accounts mentioned, to be perhaps the darlings and “ingentia decora,” or the glory, of their party. If thoughts of this secretly creep into their hearts and move them to more than ordinary diligence and activity in their way and profession, they are entangled. Instead of aiming at more glory, they need to lie in the dust, with a sense of their own vileness. And this temptation clings so close that often it needs no food to feed on except that the man entangled in it avoids all means and ways of honor and reputation, as long as it can whisper in his heart that this very avoidance is honorable. The same may be the condition of men, as I said, in #emph[preaching the gospel], in the work of the ministry. Many things in that work may bring them esteem: their ability, their plainness, their frequent preaching, their success. And in this sense all of these may be fuel for temptations. Let a man know, then, that when he likes what feeds his lust, and keeps it up by ways that are either good in themselves or not downright sinful, he has entered into temptation.

(4.) Suppose that through a man’s state or condition of life, or by any means whatever, it happens that his lust and some temptation meet with occasions and opportunities to provoke and stir it up. Let that man know, whether he perceives it or not, that he has certainly entered into temptation. I told you before that to enter into temptation is not merely to be tempted, but to be so under its #emph[power] as to be entangled by it. Now, it is almost impossible for a man to have opportunities, occasions, and advantages suited to his lust and corruption and not be entangled. If ambassadors come from the king of Babylon, Hezekiah’s pride will throw him into temptation. If Hazael is king of Syria, his cruelty and ambition will make him rage savagely against Israel. If the priests come with their pieces of silver, Judas’s covetousness will instantly be at work to sell his Master. And many instances of the same kind may be given in the days in which we live. Some men think they can play at the hole of the asp and not be stung, touch pitch and not be defiled, take fire into their clothes and not be burned. But they will be mistaken. If your business, way of life, company, or anything else of that kind throws you upon such things, ways, and persons as suit your lust or corruption, know that you have entered into temptation; how you will come out, God only knows. Suppose a man who has any seeds of filthiness in his heart is engaged, in the course of his life, in light, empty, and foolish company. Whatever notice he takes of it, little, great, or none at all, he has undoubtedly entered into temptation. So it is with ambition in high places, with passion in a crowd of perplexing affairs, and with a polluted, corrupt imagination in empty company and in the reading of idle books or writings of vanity and folly. Fire and things that burn could more easily be brought to lie together without affecting each other than #emph[particular] lusts and #emph[suitable] objects or occasions for their exercise.

(5.) Suppose a man who used to have another frame is #emph[weakened], made #emph[negligent] or #emph[formal] in duty, so that he can omit duties, or content himself with a careless, lifeless performance of them, without delight, joy, or satisfaction to his soul. Let him know that, though he may not be aware of the particular disorder it consists in, he has entered into temptation in something or other; and in the end he will find it plain, to his trouble and peril. How many have we seen and known in our days who have fallen from a warm profession into being negligent, careless, and indifferent in praying, reading, hearing, and the like! Give one instance of a man who has come off without a wound, and I dare say you may find a hundred against him who have shown that they were asleep on the top of the mast, that they were in the jaws of some vile temptation or other, which later brought forth bitter fruit in their lives and ways. From some few who return from folly we hear these mournful complaints every day: “Oh! I neglected private prayer; I did not meditate on the word or attend to hearing it, but rather despised these things. And yet I said I was rich and needed nothing. Little did I consider that this unclean lust was ripening in my heart, that this atheism and these abominations were brewing there.” This is a certain rule: if the heart of a man who has had another frame grows cold, negligent, or formal in the duties of the worship of God, whether as to their matter or their manner, one temptation or another has laid hold of him. The world, or pride, or uncleanness, or self-seeking, or malice and envy, or something or other has taken possession of his spirit. #emph[His hair is streaked with gray], though he does not perceive it. And this is to be observed as to the manner of duties as well as their matter. Men may keep up and frequent the duties of religion, as to their substance and matter, for many crooked reasons, especially to satisfy their consciences, when they have no heart for them and no life in them, as to the spirituality required in performing them. Sardis kept up the performance of duties and so had a reputation for being alive; but it lacked spiritual life in performing them, and so it was “dead” (Rev 3:1). It is as with disorders of the body. If a man finds his spirits faint, his heart oppressed, his head heavy, and his whole body out of sorts, then, though he does not yet actually burn with fever or rave, he will cry, “I fear I am coming down with a fever, I am so out of order and unwell.” A man may do the same in this sickness of the soul. If he finds that his pulse does not beat rightly and evenly toward the duties of worship and communion with God, if his spirit is low and his heart faint in them, let him conclude that he has entered into temptation, though his lust does not yet burn or rage. It is high time for him to consider the particular causes of his disorder. If the head is heavy and slumbers in the things of grace, if the heart is cold in duties, evil lies at the door. And if such a soul escapes a great temptation to sin, yet it will not escape a great temptation by desertion. The bride cries, “I sleep” (Song 5:2), and says that she had taken off her robe and could not put it on again; she was unwilling for duties and communion with Christ. What is the next news you have of her? “My beloved had turned and gone” (verse 6). Christ was gone, and she seeks him a long time and does not find him. There is such a fitness between the new nature worked and created in believers and the duties of the worship of God that they will not be parted or kept apart, unless it is by some disturbing disorder coming between them. The new creature feeds on these duties, is strengthened and increased by them, and finds sweetness in them. Indeed, in them it meets its God and Father. So of itself it cannot help delighting in them and desiring to be exercised in them, unless it is made sick by some temptation. This frame is described throughout the 119th Psalm. I say, the new creature is not thrown out of this frame and temper unless it is oppressed and disordered by one secret temptation or another. There are various other evidences of a soul’s entering into temptation, which it may discover by inquiring.

I set this forth to remove the false #emph[security] we are prone to fall into, and to show what the special duty is that we are to apply ourselves to in the special seasons of temptation. For the man who has already entered into temptation is to apply himself to the means of getting free, not to labor to keep from entering in. How this may be done I will declare later.
